Say what the root nub is called and what the tree holds

The root nub is the tree's root entry: InitIOKit() hands the tree to
IOPlatformExpertDevice::init(), which makes a registry entry out of it and
gives that entry the nub's name.  So the name says whether the tree was
found at all.  If it is not "device-tree", then nothing that matches on
that name is ever offered the nub -- the platform expert that reads the
tree never starts, the nodes under /arm-io are never published, and the
storage controller is never matched.  That is the whole chain, and the name
is where it can be seen from.

The nub's children are the tree's children, so the two lookups below say
whether the branch the storage driver needs is in the tree and whether the
interrupt controller is.

The registry is used rather than SecureDTLookupEntry(), because the device
tree reader's types live in a header that only the platform expert sees,
and this file is not that file.

The lines land next to the personality line, because that is where the
matching starts and because the output reaches the screen there.  Earlier
in the boot there is nowhere to print: the console is not up yet, and what
the kernel writes to the ramoops ring does not reach the panel.

---

diff --git a/iokit/Kernel/IOStartIOKit.cpp b/iokit/Kernel/IOStartIOKit.cpp
--- a/iokit/Kernel/IOStartIOKit.cpp
+++ b/iokit/Kernel/IOStartIOKit.cpp
@@ -245,6 +245,66 @@
 
 			printf("\nnio: publishing %d static personalities",
 			       (int) personalities->getCount());
+			/* nio: what the tree actually holds, now that it is being
+			 * read for the first time.
+			 *
+			 * The root nub is the tree's root entry: InitIOKit() hands
+			 * the tree to IOPlatformExpertDevice::init(), which makes a
+			 * registry entry out of it and gives that entry the nub's
+			 * name.  So the name says whether the tree was found at all
+			 * -- if it is not "device-tree", then nothing that matches on
+			 * that name is ever offered the nub, the platform expert that
+			 * reads the tree never starts, the nodes under /arm-io are
+			 * never published, and the storage controller is never
+			 * matched.  That is the whole chain, and the name is where it
+			 * can be seen from.
+			 *
+			 * The nub's children are the tree's children, so the two
+			 * lookups below say whether the branch the storage driver
+			 * needs is in the tree and whether the interrupt controller
+			 * is.
+			 *
+			 * The registry is used rather than SecureDTLookupEntry(),
+			 * because the device tree reader's types live in a header
+			 * that only the platform expert sees, and this file is not
+			 * that file.
+			 *
+			 * The lines land next to the personality line, because that is
+			 * where the matching starts and because the output reaches the
+			 * screen there.  Earlier in the boot there is nowhere to
+			 * print: the console is not up yet, and what the kernel
+			 * writes to the ramoops ring does not reach the panel.
+			 */
+			{
+				int hasArmIo = 0;
+				int hasIntc  = 0;
+				int hasUfs   = 0;
+				OSIterator *children = gRootNub->getChildIterator(gIODTPlane);
+				OSObject *object;
+				while (children != NULL && (object = children->getNextObject()) != NULL) {
+					IORegistryEntry *node = OSDynamicCast(IORegistryEntry, object);
+					const char *name = node ? node->getName() : NULL;
+					if (name == NULL || strcmp(name, "arm-io") != 0) {
+						continue;
+					}
+					hasArmIo = 1;
+					OSIterator *under = node->getChildIterator(gIODTPlane);
+					OSObject *inner;
+					while (under != NULL && (inner = under->getNextObject()) != NULL) {
+						IORegistryEntry *leaf = OSDynamicCast(IORegistryEntry, inner);
+						const char *leafName = leaf ? leaf->getName() : NULL;
+						if (leafName == NULL) {
+							continue;
+						}
+						if (strcmp(leafName, "intc") == 0) { hasIntc = 1; }
+						if (strcmp(leafName, "ufs")  == 0) { hasUfs  = 1; }
+					}
+				}
+				printf("\nnio: root nub is named %s",
+				       gRootNub->getName() ? gRootNub->getName() : "(none)");
+				printf("\nnio: tree has /arm-io=%d /arm-io/intc=%d /arm-io/ufs=%d",
+				       hasArmIo, hasIntc, hasUfs);
+			}
 			gIOCatalogue->addDrivers(personalities, false);
 		}
 	}
